---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'Hearts Strategy: How to Win (Tips & Tactics) | CardHearth';
const description =
  'Practical Hearts strategy: how to pass, how to duck the Queen of Spades, when to take points on purpose, how to read the table, and when to risk shooting the moon.';
const updated = '2026-06-14';
const heading = 'Hearts Strategy: How to Win';
const subtitle =
  'The habits that turn Hearts from luck into skill — smarter passing, ducking the Queen, controlling the lead, and knowing when to gamble on the moon.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Hearts Strategy', href: '/guides/hearts-strategy/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Should I always pass the Queen of Spades?',
    a: 'Not always. If you also hold low spades to protect her — and especially several of them — keeping the Queen lets you control when she falls. Pass her when your spades are high or thin and she is likely to land on you.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I avoid getting stuck with the Queen of Spades?',
    a: 'Keep low spades (the two through about the nine). When spades are led you can follow underneath the Queen, and you avoid being forced to win a spade trick and "catch" her. Voiding a suit early also lets you dump her on someone else.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should I try to shoot the moon?',
    a: 'Only with a genuinely dominant hand: lots of high hearts, top spades including control of the Queen, and high cards in a side suit. Watch the table — if an opponent takes even one point card, your shot is dead and you should bail out immediately.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Hearts Strategy: How to Win (Tips & Tactics)',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CardHearth' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/hearts-strategy/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og/hearts.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/hearts/', label: 'Play Hearts' }}
>
  <p>
    Hearts rewards patience and planning far more than luck. If you already know
    <a href="/guides/hearts-rules/">the rules</a>, the difference between a good player and a
    point-magnet comes down to a handful of habits: passing well, managing the Queen of Spades,
    creating voids, and reading when to duck versus when to strike. Here is how to win more.
  </p>

  <h2>Pass with a plan</h2>
  <p>
    The pass is your most important decision, made before you see anything in return. Aim to make
    your hand <strong>safe</strong>, not just to dump your worst three cards. Good candidates to pass
    are high hearts (the Ace, King, Queen and Jack), and the dangerous spades — the
    <strong>Ace and King of Spades</strong>, which can force you to capture the Queen. If you are
    going to keep the Queen of Spades, keep enough <strong>low spades</strong> to guard her.
  </p>

  <h2>Respect the Queen of Spades</h2>
  <p>
    Thirteen points sit in one card, so half the game is about her. Two truths drive everything:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Low spades are armour.</strong> Holding the two through nine of spades means you can
      always follow a spade lead underneath the Queen, so you are never forced to win the trick that
      catches her.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>High bare spades are a liability.</strong> The Ace or King of Spades with no low
      spades behind them is how you accidentally win the Queen. Pass them or play them off early.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When you don't have the Queen, you want to flush her out: leading spades early can force the
    holder to commit, and if you are void in spades you can drop her on whoever wins the trick.
  </p>

  <h2>Create voids early</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>void</strong> — having no cards in a suit — is a weapon. The moment a suit you are void
    in is led, you can throw away a heart or the Queen of Spades onto someone else's trick. Use the
    pass and the early tricks to shed your shortest side suit so you have somewhere to dump points
    later in the hand.
  </p>

  <h2>Duck, don't win</h2>
  <p>
    Most of the time you want to <strong>lose</strong> tricks, not win them. Play your highest
    <em>safe</em> card when you can't be caught, and save your low cards for when a suit gets
    dangerous. Counting how many cards of a suit have been played tells you when your King or Queen
    of a side suit has become safe to throw — or when it is about to win a pile of hearts you don't
    want.
  </p>

  <h2>Control the lead</h2>
  <p>
    Leading lets you steer the hand. Early on, lead suits you are <strong>strong and low</strong> in
    to draw out high cards safely. Avoid leading a suit where you hold the dangerous top card. Once
    hearts are broken, leading low hearts can bleed points onto opponents who are out of the suit.
  </p>

  <h2>Know when to shoot the moon — and when to stop one</h2>
  <p>
    Shooting the moon (taking all 26 points) flips the score: you get zero and everyone else gets 26.
    Only attempt it with a powerhouse hand — many high hearts, the Queen of Spades with protection,
    and high control cards in a side suit. Bail the instant an opponent takes a point you needed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Just as important is <strong>stopping</strong> someone else's moon. If one player is gobbling up
    every point and you can take a single heart to break their run, do it — one point now beats 26
    later. Watching for an opponent who is suspiciously winning everything is a defensive skill worth
    practising.
  </p>

  <h2>Watch the scoreboard near 100</h2>
  <p>
    As scores climb toward 100, play the table, not just your hand. If a trailing opponent is about
    to lose, you can afford to take a few points to deny a leader. And if <em>you</em> are close to
    100, every point matters — tighten up, duck relentlessly, and don't gamble on a half-hearted moon.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Put it into practice — <a href="/hearts/">play Hearts</a> against the computer. New to the game?
    Start with the <a href="/guides/hearts-rules/">Hearts rules</a>. For more trick-taking, try
    <a href="/guides/spades-strategy/">Spades strategy</a> or browse the
    <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
